----
-Question 2: FetchContent – GIT_TAG and GIT_SHALLOW
----
-
-GIT_TAG "v4.2.0":
-  Controls which commit/branch/tag of the GSL repository is checked out.
-  "v4.2.0" is a Git tag pointing to the exact release commit, ensuring
-  reproducible, pinned builds. Without it, FetchContent would default to
-  the repository's default branch (typically "main"), which may include
-  unreleased changes and break reproducibility.
-
-GIT_SHALLOW ON:
-  Performs a shallow clone (git clone --depth 1) instead of a full
-  history clone. Only the latest commit reachable from GIT_TAG is
-  fetched, without any parent history.
-
-If GIT_SHALLOW is not set (defaults to OFF):
-  A full clone of the repository is performed, downloading the entire
-  Git history (potentially thousands of commits and all branch data).
-  In CI this typically adds 30-60 seconds of extra clone time per job,
-  and for large repositories can add several minutes. Since GSL is a
-  header-only library and the build only needs the source tree at the
-  tagged commit, the full history is unnecessary. Setting GIT_SHALLOW ON
-  is the recommended practice for CI to minimize network I/O and clone
-  duration.
-
---- a/examples/fetch_content_example/CMakeLists.txt
-+++ b/examples/fetch_content_example/CMakeLists.txt
-@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
-+cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.14)
-+project(fetch_content_example LANGUAGES CXX)
-+
-+include(FetchContent)
-+
-+# GIT_TAG "v4.2.0"  →  Checks out the exact release tagged v4.2.0,
-+#                        ensuring reproducible, pinned builds.
-+#                        Without it, the default branch HEAD is used,
-+#                        which may include unreleased / breaking changes.
-+#
-+# GIT_SHALLOW ON     →  Performs a shallow clone (git clone --depth 1),
-+#                        fetching only the commit at GIT_TAG without
-+#                        the full history.
-+#                        If omitted (defaults OFF), a full clone downloads
-+#                        the entire Git history, adding ~30-60s of extra
-+#                        clone time per CI job. For a header-only library
-+#                        like GSL, the full history is unnecessary.
-+
-+FetchContent_Declare(GSL
-+    GIT_REPOSITORY "https://github.com/microsoft/GSL"
-+    GIT_TAG        "v4.2.0"
-+    GIT_SHALLOW    ON
-+)
-+
-+FetchContent_MakeAvailable(GSL)
-+
-+target_link_libraries(foobar PRIVATE Microsoft.GSL::GSL)
-
----
-Question 3: CTest command – run only "span" tests with JSON output
----
-
-Command:
-
-  ctest --output-on-failure -R span --output-junit span-results.xml -T Test --no-compress-output
-
-For native JSON output (CTest 3.21+):
-
-  ctest -R span --output-json span-results.json
-
-Explanation of flags:
-  -R span           →  Run only tests whose name matches the regex "span"
-  --output-json     →  Write test results in JSON format (requires CMake 3.21+)
-  --output-junit    →  Alternative: write JUnit-style XML (widely supported)
-
-If CMake < 3.21 is available, use the JUnit XML output and convert:
-
-  ctest -R span --output-junit span-results.xml
-  # Then convert XML to JSON with a tool like jq + xq, or use a CI plugin
-
---- a/scripts/run_span_tests.sh
-+++ b/scripts/run_span_tests.sh
-@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
-+#!/usr/bin/env bash
-+set -euo pipefail
-+
-+# Run only tests whose names contain "span", output results as JSON.
-+# Requires CMake 3.21+ for native --output-json support.
-+#
-+# Usage:
-+#   ./scripts/run_span_tests.sh [build_dir]
-+
-+BUILD_DIR="${1:-build}"
-+
-+ctest \
-+  --test-dir "${BUILD_DIR}" \
-+  -R span \
-+  --output-json "${BUILD_DIR}/span-test-results.json" \
-+  --output-on-failure
-+
-+echo "Results written to ${BUILD_DIR}/span-test-results.json"
